# HQL core

HQL is a typed functional language for querying and manipulating typed
knowledge. The core names no source: documents, relational tables and graph
stores all arrive through an [[hql/extensions|extension]], and HyperMarkDown is
one of them — see [[hmd-integration]]. The language is still being designed.
This card describes the intended core; the working bootstrap supports only Int,
Float, Bool and same-type numeric addition.

The core should stay small: typed values, bindings, ordinary functions, function
application and composition through `|`. Document and semantic types provide the
domain vocabulary. Most operations should be functions, with external capabilities
supplied by [[hql/extensions]]. HQL should develop its own functional syntax,
without adopting SQL clauses or Rust-like trait machinery.

## Value vocabulary

| Family | Types | Role |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Documents | `Doc`, `Card`, `Section`, `Link` | Authored knowledge and its structure |
| Semantic | `Concept`, `Relation`, `Proposition`, `Evidence` | Meaning, claims and support |
| Collections | `Set<T>`, `Seq<T>`, `Map<K, V>` | Membership, order and keyed lookup — see [[hql/collections]] |
| Ordinary | `String`, `Int`, `Float`, `Bool`, `Date` | General computation |

These are the current design names. Generic types are written with angle
brackets, `Set<T>` and `Edge<S, T>`, everywhere, and square brackets stay
value-level: `[alice, bob]` is a sequence and `xs[0]` is indexing. Subtyping,
generic parameters and progressive refinement are [[type-system]]'s subject.
Precise schemas, declarations, polymorphism, optional/error types and Date
operations remain open.
This vocabulary does not require each type to be a primitive in the evaluator.

## Bindings and values

```hql
alice : Doc = [[alice]]
```

`[[alice]]` is a doc reference: it names a document in the current namespace and
nothing more, and the `->` form built from it is the link operator — see
[[link-op]]. The binding names the referenced document; a filename does not
implicitly introduce a variable. Inferred binding is also preferred:

```hql
alice = [[alice]]
```

A document is not yet a card. Card-ness is established by a checked narrowing,
never by the reference; see [[card-type]].

An expression returns a typed value. A program returns its last expression;
earlier unbound values are discarded. Bindings retain values for later use.
Rendering belongs to the host, and printing or saving requires an explicit
effect. Constructing an updated Card does not itself persist a document.
The spelling of a no-result type, Unit or Void, remains open.

## Context values

`cards : Set<Card>` denotes the cards in the current vault or namespace. It
does not imply a traversal order. See [[hql/collections]].

`knowledge` denotes the semantic knowledge universe composed of concepts,
relations, propositions and evidence. It is richer than a collection of cards
or a graph. See [[hql/knowledge]].

```hql
knowledge
| graph
```

Pipes are fundamental to composition and querying. [[pipes]] describes the
execution direction; [[hql/knowledge]] records the unresolved distinction between
graph projection and presentation.

## Design boundaries

The host supplies a typed document model through an extension over the existing
HMD parser and resolver. Raw HMD string searching is not the language's
foundation, and neither is HMD the foundation: the document vocabulary belongs
to the extension that supplies documents.
Namespace imports belong in [[hql/extensions]], including optional `init.hql`.

The design baseline and unresolved choices are collected in [[hql/design-status]].
See also [[hql]], [[hmd-integration]] and the
[program-value model](../../models/behavior/program-values.md).
